     1	# Zeugma
     2	
     3	A parametric fuzzer that uses call tree information to select crossover points.
     4	
     5	## Requirements
     6	
     7	* OpenJDK 11
     8	* [Apache Maven](https://maven.apache.org/) 3.6.0+
     9	
    10	## Building
    11	
    12	1. Ensure that some version of OpenJDK 11 is installed.
    13	2. Set the JAVA_HOME environmental variable to the path of this OpenJDK 11 installation.
    14	   For example, on Linux, run `export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64`.
    15	3. Clone or download this repository.
    16	4. In the root directory for this project, run `mvn -DskipTests install`.
    17	
    18	## Running a Fuzzing Campaign
    19	
    20	After this project has been built, you can run and analyze a fuzzing campaign.
    21	In the root directory of this project, run the following command:
    22	
    23	```
    24	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-tools \
    25	  meringue:fuzz meringue:analyze \
    26	  -P<SUBJECT>,<FUZZER> \
    27	  -Dmeringue.outputDirectory=<OUTPUT_DIRECTORY> \
    28	  -Dmeringue.duration=<DURATION>
    29	```
    30	
    31	Where:
    32	
    33	* \<SUBJECT\> is the fuzzing target: ant, bcel, closure, maven, nashorn, rhino, tomcat.
    34	* \<FUZZER\> is the fuzzer to be used: bedivfuzz-simple, bedivfuzz-structure, rlcheck, zest, zeugma-linked, zeugma-none,
    35	  zeugma-one_point, or zeugma-two_point.
    36	* \<OUTPUT_DIRECTORY\> is the path of the directory to which campaign output files should be written.
    37	  If a relative path is used, then the path will be resolved relative to the "zeugma-evaluation/zeugma-evaluation-tools"
    38	  directory not the project root.
    39	* \<DURATION\> is the maximum amount of time to execute the fuzzing campaign for specified in the ISO-8601 duration
    40	  format (e.g., "P2DT3H4M" represents 2 days, 3 hours, and 4 minutes).
    41	
    42	This command will first run a fuzzing campaign for the specified duration.
    43	Once this command has completed, the results of the campaign will be analyzed to collect coverage information
    44	using the [JaCoCo Java Code Coverage Library](https://www.eclemma.org/jacoco/) and information about exposed failures.
    45	After the analysis phase finishes, the output directory will contain the following files:
    46	
    47	```
    48	├── campaign
    49	│   ├── corpus
    50	│   │   └── *
    51	│   ├── failures
    52	│   │   └── *
    53	│   └── *
    54	├── jacoco
    55	│   └── jacoco.csv
    56	├── coverage.csv
    57	├── failures.json
    58	└── summary.json
    59	```
    60	
    61	The "campaign" directory stores data written by the fuzzer.
    62	This data will include a "corpus" directory containing coverage-revealing inputs saved during the fuzzing campaign and
    63	a "failures" directory containing failure-inducing inputs saved during the fuzzing campaign.
    64	
    65	When the fuzzing campaign is analyzed, inputs saved to the "corpus" and "failures" directories are run in order by
    66	timestamp to compute JaCoCo branch coverage over time, which is then saved to the file "coverage.csv".
    67	There are two columns in "coverage.csv": "time" and "covered_branches".
    68	The "time" column represents elapsed time in milliseconds since the beginning of the campaign, and the
    69	"covered_branches" column represents the number of branches covered in application classes as determined by JaCoCo.
    70	For example, if "coverage.csv" contained the row `100, 340` that would indicate that 340 branches were covered by inputs
    71	saved in the first 100 milliseconds of the campaign.
    72	
    73	The file "jacoco/jacoco.csv" is a JaCoCo-generated report detailing coverage in application classes after all the
    74	inputs saved to the "corpus" and "failures" directories have been run.
    75	
    76	The file "failures.json" contains a list of deduplicated failures that were induced by at least one input saved
    77	to "corpus" or "failures" directories.
    78	Failures are naively deduplicated using the top five elements of the failure's stack trace and the type of exception
    79	thrown (e.g., `java.lang.IndexOutOfBoundsException`).
    80	Each entry in "failures.json" will contain the top five elements (or fewer if the trace contains fewer than five
    81	elements) of the stack trace for the failure, the type of exception thrown, the elapsed time in milliseconds since the
    82	beginning of the campaign when the first input that induced the failure was saved, the message (as returned by
    83	`java.lang.Throwable#getMessage`) for the first instance of the failure, and a list of saved inputs
    84	that induced the failure.
    85	
    86	The file "summary.json" contains information about the configuration used for the fuzzing campaign, such as the Java
    87	options that were used and the fuzzing target.
    88	
    89	## Replaying an Input
    90	
    91	After this project has been built and a fuzzing campaign has been completed, you can rerun any input that was saved by
    92	the fuzzer.
    93	In the root directory of this project, run the following command:
    94	
    95	```
    96	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-tools \
    97	  meringue:replay \
    98	  -P<SUBJECT>,<FUZZER> \
    99	  -Dmeringue.input=<INPUT>
   100	```
   101	
   102	Where:
   103	
   104	* \<SUBJECT\> is the fuzzing target for the input to be replayed: ant, bcel, closure, maven,
   105	  nashorn, rhino, tomcat.
   106	* \<FUZZER\> is the fuzzer that was used to produce the input to be replayed: bedivfuzz-simple, bedivfuzz-structure,
   107	  rlcheck, zest, zeugma-linked, zeugma-none, zeugma-one_point, or zeugma-two_point.
   108	* \<INPUT\> is the path of a file containing an input that was saved during a fuzzing campaign performed by the
   109	  specified fuzzer on the specified subject.
   110	
   111	This command will start a Java process to rerun the input.
   112	This process will stop and wait for a debugger to attach on port 5005.
   113	If the input being rerun throws an exception, that exception's stack trace will be printed to standard err.
   114	
   115	## Computing Heritability Metrics
   116	
   117	After this project has been built and one or more fuzzing campaigns have been completed using the fuzzer zeugma-none,
   118	you can compute the heritability of one-point, two-point, and linked crossover.
   119	First, create an input directory containing the campaigns to be used to compute crossover heritability.
   120	This directory should contain gzipped TAR archives of the output directories (see the OUTPUT_DIRECTORY argument
   121	described in ["Running a Fuzzing Campaign"](#Running-a-Fuzzing-Campaign)) from the fuzzing campaigns to be included.
   122	The archives' names should end with the extension ".tgz" and can be in subdirectories of the input directory.
   123	For example, the following is a valid structure for the input directory:
   124	
   125	```
   126	├── campaign-0
   127	│   └── meringue.tgz
   128	├── campaign-1
   129	│   └── meringue.tgz
   130	└── campaign-2
   131	    └── meringue.tgz
   132	```
   133	
   134	The input directory should not contain any file with the extension ".tgz" that is not a gzipped TAR archive of a
   135	campaign output directory.
   136	Once you have built the input directory, in the root directory of this project, run the following command:
   137	
   138	```
   139	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-heritability \
   140	    -Pcompute install \
   141	    -Dheritability.corpora=<INPUT_DIRECTORY> \
   142	    -Dheritability.output=<OUTPUT_FILE>
   143	```
   144	
   145	Where:
   146	
   147	* \<INPUT_DIRECTORY\> is the path of the directory to be scanned for fuzzing campaign archives. If a relative path is
   148	  used, then the path will be resolved relative to the zeugma-evaluation/zeugma-evaluation-heritability directory, not
   149	  the project root.
   150	* \<OUTPUT_FILE\> is the path of the file to which the results should be written in CSV format.
   151	  If a relative path is used, then the path will be resolved relative to the
   152	  zeugma-evaluation/zeugma-evaluation-heritability directory, not the project root.
   153	
   154	## Creating a Report
   155	
   156	After this project has been built and one or more fuzzing campaigns have been completed, you can create a report
   157	summarizing the results of those campaigns.
   158	First, create an input directory containing the results of the campaigns to be included in the report.
   159	This input directory should contain one subdirectory for each campaign to be included in the report.
   160	Each of these subdirectories should contain the "coverage.csv", "failures.json", and "summary.json" output files for a
   161	campaign (see ["Running a Fuzzing Campaign"](#Running-a-Fuzzing-Campaign) for information about these files).
   162	If you wish to include heritability results in the report, add the heritability CSV (see the OUTPUT_FILE argument
   163	described in ["Computing Heritability Metrics"](#Computing-Heritability-Metrics)) to the input directory with the
   164	name "heritability.csv".
   165	For example, the following is a valid structure for the input directory:
   166	
   167	```
   168	├── campaign-0
   169	│   ├── coverage.csv
   170	│   ├── failures.json
   171	│   └── summary.json
   172	├── campaign-1
   173	│   ├── coverage.csv
   174	│   ├── failures.json
   175	│   └── summary.json
   176	├── campaign-2
   177	│   ├── coverage.csv
   178	│   ├── failures.json
   179	│   └── summary.json
   180	└── heritability.csv
   181	```
   182	
   183	Next, ensure that you have Python 3.9+ installed.
   184	In the root directory of this project, create and activate a virtual environment:
   185	
   186	```
   187	python3 -m venv venv
   188	. venv/bin/activate
   189	```
   190	
   191	Now install the required libraries:
   192	
   193	```
   194	python3 -m pip install -r ./resources/requirements.txt
   195	```
   196	
   197	Finally, create the report by running:
   198	
   199	```
   200	python3 scripts/report.py <INPUT_DIRECTORY> <OUTPUT_FILE>
   201	```
   202	
   203	Where:
   204	
   205	* \<INPUT_DIRECTORY\> is the path of the input directory you created.
   206	* \<OUTPUT_FILE\> is the path of the file to which the report should be written in HTML format.
